---
args: { input: CaseInput }
returns: RunProposal
---
1. Bind `goal = await frameRequest(input)` from the request and `policy = await selectPolicy(input)` from supplied authority candidates. Preserve unresolved precedence instead of guessing which source governs.
2. Bind `work = await formTaskGraph(goal)` and `evidencePlan = await planEvidence(input, work)` so each operation has explicit prerequisites and a searched source universe.
3. Run the stages in their dependency order. The first stage receives only `input.domainRecords.firstStage`; every later stage receives the preceding function's actual accepted records. Bind each full result separately.
const interpretResponsesResult = await interpretResponses(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan);
const formHypothesesResult = await formHypotheses(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan, interpretResponsesResult.accepted);
const generateDiagnosticsResult = await generateDiagnostics(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan, formHypothesesResult.accepted);
const planRepairResult = await planRepair(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan, generateDiagnosticsResult.accepted);
const verifyTransferResult = await verifyTransfer(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan, planRepairResult.accepted);
4. Bind `stageSet: CaseStageSet = { interpretResponses: interpretResponsesResult, formHypotheses: formHypothesesResult, generateDiagnostics: generateDiagnosticsResult, planRepair: planRepairResult, verifyTransfer: verifyTransferResult }` and `collection: CollectionFinding = await reconcileCollection(stageSet)`. Bind `allQuestions: string[] = [...interpretResponsesResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...formHypothesesResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...generateDiagnosticsResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...planRepairResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...verifyTransferResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...collection.coverageGaps]` and `questions: QuestionBatch = await resolveQuestions(allQuestions, input)`.
5. Bind `invalidation = await assessDelta(input)`, `review = await prepareReview(collection)`, and `view = await composeView(review, input)`; pass each child its actual declared inputs and retain the returned object.
6. Bind `allChecks: CheckResult[] = [...interpretResponsesResult.checks, ...formHypothesesResult.checks, ...generateDiagnosticsResult.checks, ...planRepairResult.checks, ...verifyTransferResult.checks]`. Bind `hasGateFailure = allChecks.some(check => check.verdict !== "pass")` and `hasBlocker = input.authorityStatus !== "authorized" || questions.questions.length > 0 || collection.coverageGaps.length > 0`. Bind `disposition: "proposed" | "blocked" | "needs-review" = hasBlocker ? "blocked" : hasGateFailure ? "needs-review" : "proposed"`. Bind `rationale: string[] = allChecks.map(check => `${check.check}: ${check.verdict}; evidence ${check.evidenceIds.join(",")}`)`.
7. Operational detail 1 — A learner gives a correct unconventional solution: assess its meaning rather than matching wording. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (assess its meaning rather than matching wording.) through the existing stage result for ResponseObservation; preserve exact source/version and the prior ResponseObservation, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
8. Operational detail 2 — A task contains ambiguous language: repair the task before diagnosing the learner. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (repair the task before diagnosing the learner.) through the existing stage result for ResponseObservation; preserve exact source/version and the prior ResponseObservation, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
9. Operational detail 3 — A session is interrupted: resume from the last completed task without revealing hidden diagnostic labels. When supplied evidence contains a session checkpoint, bind the latest verified checkpoint, its exact completed-task receipts, the pending-task cursor, the requesting audience and access policy, and the current effect-ledger entries from those inputs. Preserve every completed receipt and select only the first task that has no start or completion receipt; do not replay completed tasks. Construct the audience-specific view only after enforcing the supplied role permissions, omitting any restricted diagnostic label or private annotation from an unauthorized audience. Before proposing a resume notification or continuation, look up the exact idempotency key and receipt in the supplied effect ledger; if it is already delivered, do not enqueue it again, otherwise propose a scoped continuation bound to that same key and the checkpoint version. Preserve checkpoint history, present the continuation as an approval-bound proposal, and do not claim a session write or dispatch. Resolve all identifiers from the supplied records; do not assume task names, IDs, statuses, or keys.
10. Operational detail 4 — An accommodation changes presentation: preserve the competency being assessed. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (preserve the competency being assessed.) through the existing stage result for ResponseObservation; preserve exact source/version and the prior ResponseObservation, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
11. Operational detail 5 — Repeated failure causes frustration: offer a bounded instructor handoff rather than an endless loop. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (offer a bounded instructor handoff rather than an endless loop.) through the existing stage result for ResponseObservation; preserve exact source/version and the prior ResponseObservation, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
12. Initialize `revisions: RevisionProposal[] = []`. If any gate is not pass, bind `revision = await proposeRevision(the failed check names, the smallest affected stage name)` and add that returned proposal to revisions. Bind and return `result: RunProposal = { caseId: input.caseId, goal, policy, work, evidencePlan, interpretResponses: interpretResponsesResult, formHypotheses: formHypothesesResult, generateDiagnostics: generateDiagnosticsResult, planRepair: planRepairResult, verifyTransfer: verifyTransferResult, collection, questions, invalidation, review, view, revisions, disposition, rationale }`. Proposed actions remain proposals. The host owns authorization, persistence, effect dispatch and verification.
